Experiment: Analyse Various Types of Servers Using Webalizer Tool

Aim
To analyse web server log files using the Webalizer tool and study website traffic, visitor statistics, requests, and data transfer.

Algorithm / Procedure
1. Install the Apache web server and Webalizer tool.
2. Generate or collect web server access log files.
3. Configure Webalizer to read the server log file.
4. Run Webalizer to analyse the log data.
5. Generate HTML reports containing traffic statistics.
6. Open the generated report in a web browser.
7. Analyse requests, visitors, URLs, and bandwidth usage.

Commands (Ubuntu/Linux)
1. Install Webalizer
sudo apt update
sudo apt install apache2 webalizer

2. Generate server traffic

Open a browser and visit http://localhost several times.

3. Analyse the Apache access log
sudo webalizer /var/log/apache2/access.log

sudo webalizer /var/log/apache2/access.log


4. View the generated report

Webalizer normally generates index.html and related report files in its configured output directory. Check the configuration:
cat /etc/webalizer/webalizer.conf

Types of Servers Analysed
Server type	Purpose	Example analysis
Web server	Hosts websites	Page requests, visitors, bandwidth
FTP server	Transfers files	Downloads and file requests, if logged
Proxy server	Forwards client requests	Requests and traffic, if compatible logs are available
Mail server	Sends and receives email	Mail activity, if compatible logs are available
Application server	Runs application logic	HTTP requests and responses, if logged
Note: Webalizer primarily analyses web server access logs. Other server types can be analysed only when their logs are available in a compatible format or converted appropriately.
Sample Output
